<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Utpala dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">855 CE – 939 CE (approx.) · capital Avantipura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Kashmir dynasty of Avantivarman, whose engineer Suyya tamed the Jhelum and whose court fostered great Sanskrit literary theory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 855 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avantivarman">Avantivarman</a>, a grandson of a minister of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karkota-dynasty">Karkota dynasty</a>, took the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> with the support of powerful ministers, founding a new dynasty after a period of weak Karkota rule. The main source for the dynasty is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Kalhana's Rajatarangini</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avantivarman">Avantivarman's</a> minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyya">Suyya</a> cleared rocks from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>, drained marshes and built embankments, increasing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> production and lowering grain prices, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a> records. The king built the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avantipur">Avantipur</a>, and the critic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anandavardhana">Anandavardhana</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhvanyaloka">Dhvanyaloka</a>, a founding text of Indian aesthetics, during his reign. His son Shankaravarman campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> but became notorious for heavy taxation and plunder of temples.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Shankaravarman the dynasty was dominated by the palace guards known as the Tantrins and by rival factions. In 939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashaskara">Yashaskara</a> as king, and the throne later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/parvagupta-dynasty">Parvagupta dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/utpala-dynasty.jpg" alt="Utpala dynasty" width="320" height="158"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sankaravarman,_Dupatalas_(Kashmir)_Circa_883-902_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>855 CE – 939 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Avantipura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Avantivarman, Shankaravarman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism, Vaishnavism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Utpalas of Kashmir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=897&amp;focus=utpala-dynasty" aria-label="Utpala dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6592 948 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Utpala dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6912 1204L6905 1198M6912 1204L6905 1198"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6905 1198h0M6905 1198h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6912 1204h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6912 1204h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=897&amp;focus=utpala-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
